      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "To make the pesto: put the basil, garlic and a pinch of salt into a blender or food processor. Add the olive oil. Blend until roughly combined but not completely smooth. You want some texture."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Transfer to a bowl. Stir in the grated Parmesan. Taste and adjust the salt. If you have pine nuts, toast 30g in a dry pan and add them to the blender at step 1 -- they are traditional but expensive. Omit them freely."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook the pasta in well-salted boiling water until al dente. Reserve a large mug of pasta water. Drain the pasta."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Put the pasta into a large bowl. Add 3 tablespoons of warm pasta water to the pesto and stir. Pour over the pasta and toss well. Add more pasta water as needed to make a loose, coating sauce."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve immediately with extra Parmesan grated over the top."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Pesto has turned dark and brown",
              "cause": "Basil was heated or bruised too roughly",
              "fix": "Keep pesto cool. Blanch the basil in boiling water for 5 seconds and refresh in iced water before blending -- this locks in the green colour."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Pesto is too thick and clumpy",
              "cause": "Not enough oil or pasta water",
              "fix": "Add more warm pasta water a tablespoon at a time and toss until smooth."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pesto is from Genoa on the Ligurian coast. The classic version uses small-leaf Genoese basil and pine nuts. The rest of Italy uses bigger basil and still gets good results. It is a summer sauce -- made when basil is abundant."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
